.TH GO-MCP-HOOKS 7 "2026-04-10" "go-mcp 0.0.9" "Go MCP Framework"
.SH NAME
go-mcp-hooks \- per-package PreToolUse hook system for tool interception
.SH DESCRIPTION
Packages that declare
.B MapsTools
on their commands automatically get per-package PreToolUse hooks.
The hooks intercept built-in Claude Code tools (Bash, Read, Grep, etc.)
and deny them when a matching MCP tool exists, suggesting the MCP tool
instead.
.SH HOW IT WORKS
.B GenerateAll
(and
.BR GenerateAllWithSkills )
calls
.BR GenerateHooks ,
which:
.IP 1. 3
Collects the unique set of
.B Replaces
values from all
.B ToolMapping
declarations across commands.
.IP 2. 3
Builds an alphabetically-sorted matcher string (e.g., "Bash|Grep|Read").
.IP 3. 3
Writes
.I hooks/hooks.json
with a PreToolUse entry pointing to an executable wrapper.
.IP 4. 3
Writes
.I hooks/pre-tool-use
\(em a shell script that calls
.BI < binary >" hook" .
.PP
The
.I hooks/hooks.json
uses
.B ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}
for portable path resolution:
.PP
.nf
{
  "hooks": {
    "PreToolUse": [
      {
        "matcher": "Bash",
        "hooks": [
          {
            "type": "command",
            "command": "${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/pre-tool-use",
            "timeout": 5
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
.fi
.SH WIRING THE HOOK SUBCOMMAND
Each package binary needs a
.B hook
subcommand that calls
.BR app.HandleHook .
The
.I pre-tool-use
wrapper script delegates to this subcommand.
.PP
Flag-based CLI pattern:
.PP
.nf
if flag.NArg() >= 1 && flag.Arg(0) == "hook" {
    if err := app.HandleHook(os.Stdin, os.Stdout); err != nil {
        log.Fatalf("handling hook: %v", err)
    }
    return
}
.fi
.PP
command.App pattern:
.PP
.nf
app.AddCommand(&command.Command{
    Name:   "hook",
    Hidden: true,
    Description: command.Description{
        Short: "Handle PreToolUse hook",
    },
    RunCLI: func(ctx context.Context,
        args json.RawMessage) error {
        return app.HandleHook(os.Stdin, os.Stdout)
    },
})
.fi
.SH HANDLEHOOK BEHAVIOR
.B HandleHook
reads hook input JSON from stdin, matches against all registered
.B ToolMapping
declarations, and:
.IP \(bu 2
.B Match found:
writes a deny response with the MCP tool name
(e.g.,
.IR mcp__plugin_grit_grit__status ).
.IP \(bu 2
.B No match:
writes nothing (implicit allow).
.PP
The MCP tool name follows the pattern
.IR mcp__plugin_{name}_{name}__{command} .
.PP
Hooks follow a fail-open model: errors return success, never deny.
.SH ARCHITECTURE
Hooks are entirely per-package. There is no central
.B purse-first hook
command or shared hook handler. Each package binary handles its own
PreToolUse invocations independently. This means:
.IP \(bu 2
Adding a new package with mappings automatically gets hooks.
.IP \(bu 2
Removing a package removes its hooks.
.IP \(bu 2
No coordination needed between packages.
.SH MACOS PATH RESOLUTION
On macOS,
.I /var
is a symlink to
.I /private/var
and
.I /tmp
to
.IR /private/tmp .
.BR filepath.EvalSymlinks (3)
fails on non-existent paths, returning the unresolved form.
.PP
When resolving paths that may not exist (e.g., for prefix-matching
in PreToolUse hooks), walk up the directory tree to find an existing
ancestor, resolve symlinks there, then re-append the non-existent
suffix. On Linux this is a no-op.
.PP
Always resolve
.B both
sides of a path comparison, not just one.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR go-mcp (7),
.BR go-mcp-command (7),
.BR go-mcp-purse (7)
